#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Rayburst is a Tauri v2 (WebKitGTK) app staged by apply_extra to
# /app/extra/rayburst. The GUI binary links only against libraries in
# org.gnome.Platform (webkit2gtk-4.1, gtk-3, libsoup-3, cairo, dbus, glib); its
# Linux tray is ksni, which speaks D-Bus itself and loads no indicator library.
# Its Aria2 Next
# engine sidecar sits next to it in bin/, and Tauri resolves the resource tree
# the engine reads (the peer blocklist, the GeoIP database, the ED2K bootstrap
# caches) as <exe_dir>/../lib/Rayburst, which apply_extra staged as a sibling.
#
# Flatpak redirects the XDG base dirs, so the task database, the settings and the
# engine's session state stay inside the sandbox.

# No WebKitGTK rendering variables here, unlike most Tauri packages. Since
# 4.1.2 the app sets them itself before GTK starts (src-tauri/src/webkit.rs):
# it removes WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER, forces the DMA-BUF renderer in
# shared-memory mode, and paints on the CPU unless the in-app "hardware
# rendering" preference is on, so anything exported here is overwritten. The
# one variable it leaves alone is WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE, and that
# must stay unset: the window is `transparent: true` and, on
# org.gnome.Platform//51, turning compositing off leaves it fully see-through
# onto the desktop. Measured with 4.1.2 on GNOME 51 (2026-11-10 runtime),
# native Wayland:
#
#   nothing set (the app decides)       renders
#   DISABLE_DMABUF=1                    renders (the app removes it)
#   DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1          transparent, whatever DMABUF is
#
# Earlier releases had a gpu_guard that re-exec'd itself and needed both
# variables pre-set to 0; that code is gone.

# No LD_PRELOAD getpid shim here, unlike com.getupnote.UpNote, even though the
# ksni tray does claim org.kde.StatusNotifierItem-<getpid()>-1 (granted as -2-1
# in the manifest). The shim would leak into the aria2-next engine this process
# spawns, and getpid shims have broken other apps here (docs/reference/
# incidents.md); the PID-2 contention is the smaller risk until upstream skips
# the well-known name in a sandbox.

# apply_extra recorded the launcher name from the .deb's own desktop entry, so a
# rename upstream cannot leave this wrapper pointing at a binary that is gone.
launcher="$(cat /app/extra/rayburst/.launcher)"

exec "/app/extra/rayburst/bin/$launcher" "$@"
